  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n   The central idea is to minimize the evaluation function score = 2*n*max(conv(a,a))/(sum(a))^2 by iteratively refining the sequence coefficients. Unlike the previous program that used LP-based fictitious play, we will employ a gradient-based optimization with projected gradient steps. This approach is preferable because:\n   - The auto-convolution is differentiable, allowing direct computation of gradients\n   - Projected gradient handles constraints naturally by projecting away from the boundary\n   - We can use the convex structure of the auto-convolution to derive analytic expressions\n   \n   The algorithm uses:\n   - Mode-specific auto-convolution for gradients (full mode)\n   - Weighted logarithmic smoothing for the non-differentiable max term\n   - Armijo Goldstein backtracking for adaptive step sizes\n   - Probability simplex projection to maintain non-negativity\n\n   We address the search surface by reformulating the evaluation function as a ratio of two quadratic forms. The key advantages are:\n   - Clear gradient computations without numerical differentiation\n   - Efficient handling of constraints through projection\n   - Better theoretical convergence properties\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n   We represent sequences as real vectors a \u2208 \u211d\u207a\u207f\u207f\u207a\u207f\u207a\n   The normed evaluation function is:\n   f(a) = n * max_{k=0}^{2n-2} \u2211_{i+j=k} a_i_a_j / (\u2211_{i}\u2070\u207f a_i / \u2211_{i}\u207f a_i)\n   \n   We use a surrogate of the form:\n   F(a) = n * \u2211_{k} max(0, exp( max_conv_k / T )) / (\u2211 a_i / scale)\n   where T is a smoothing parameter and scale is chosen to maintain normalization\n\n   Constraints:\n   a_i \u2265 0 for all i\n   \u2211 a_i = 1 (for normalized sequences)\n   The optimization domain is the positive orthant with simplex projection\n\n   The gradient for the auto-convolution is computed via:\n   g = \u2211_{k} (a_k - a_0)/\u03b5 * eta(k) for each component, \n   where eta(k) is the kernel of the auto-convolution at position k.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n   We initialize with a random sequence normalized to unit sum. For each optimization iteration:\n   a. Compute full convolution using numpy\n   b. Find the maximum value (with a small smoothing to avoid discontinuities)\n   c. Compute gradient:\n      - For each term in the convolution, delta function for indices\n      - Backward pass to accumulate gradients\n   d. Apply stochastic gradient projection (not deterministic) to introduce randomness\n   e. Normalize with simplex projection\n   f. Score evaluation via direct computation\n   g. Store the best candidate found and its score\n\n   We use:\n   - Armijo Goldstein condition for step-size selection\n   - \u03b3 = 0.3 cooling parameter to maintain sufficient decay\n   - \u03b2 = 0.9 momentum to bring along-gradient terms\n   - Optional curvature term to handle negative curvature\n   - Exponential smoothing with base e (approximately 2.718) and parameter 0.3 to improve condition number\n\n   The algorithm repeats until time exceeds the budget. At each position in the sequence, we allow small perturbations and choose the gradient component with the most negative impact on the score.\n\n4. Search Choices and Variants:\n   We limit the sequence length to 1024 (as larger sequences would slow down computation)\n   Hyperparameter settings:\n   - Momentum decay: fixed at 0.9 for all steps\n   - Step size: adaptive via backtracking\n   - Smoothing temperature: set based on gradient norm to balance adjustment vs. small steps\n   \n   We introduce randomness to prevent local minima:\n   - Projected gradient during descent introduces stochastic elements\n   - Each proposal includes noise proportional to the movement step size (0.3e-4)\n\n   We store the best candidate seen and evaluate its score at least once per optimization step\n   Fallback strategy: return the candidate with the smallest evaluated score\n\n5. Compute Budget and Timeliness:\n   Time estimation: \n   - Real-time evaluation using clock\n   - Backtracking loop with progress prints\n   - Automatically abort when time exceeds\n   One optimization pass: 1024 coordinates with projected gradient steps, requires 5 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 1.2 seconds (approximately 12 seconds) assuming 2.4 GHz processor\n   Concurrency management avoids parallel calls to prevent race conditions\n\n   Validation every 10 optimization steps, and the first evaluation.\n   We set timeout explicitly using the allocated time for each segment.\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n   After each optimization step, we evaluate the candidate sequence calling evaluate_sequence() at least once\n   Termination when time_budget is exceeded (1800 seconds)\n   The gradient is defined via forward/backward passes but corrected for boundary effects due to full convolution\n\n   The evaluation function score is firmly defined as:\n   score = 2 * n * max(convolve(a, a, mode='full')) / (sum(a))^2\n   We ensure consistency between computed auto-convolution and full-mode convolution through:\n   - Double-checking all index calculations\n   - Explicitly computing and verifying the evaluated sequence\n   - Using same libraries and conventions as the original evaluator\n\n   The returned sequence is always positive and normalized to unit sum, fitting within numerical limits (1e-8 to 1000)\n   Final sequence is explicitly evaluated and stored\n\n   Special cases handling:\n   - If initial sequence is always rejected by evaluate_sequence(), start from scratch\n   - The auto-convolution matrix computed via: convolve(a, a, 'full')\n   - Auto-convolution length is fixed at 2*len(a)-1\n\n   Implementation structure:\n   We separate helper functions for:\n   - Auto-convolution computation\n   - Gradient calculation\n   - Simplex projection\n   - Score evaluation\n   - Input processing and timing\n   - Candidate generation\n\n   We initialize from the parent sequence if available, otherwise generate a complete new sequence.\n\n   The algorithm follows:\n   while time_budget > 0:\n      projection = simplex_projection(update)\n      score = evaluate_sequence(update)\n      if score improves, record candidate\n\n   where update uses the projected gradient from current estimate.\n\nDetailed walking implementation below:",
